+# Blocked: `test_feb_2100` asserts a false fact
+
+`test_billing_calendar.py::test_feb_2100` expects `days_in_month(2100, 2) == 29`
+and states in a comment that 2100 "is a leap year". That is incorrect under the
+Gregorian calendar: a year divisible by 100 is only a leap year if it is also
+divisible by 400. 2100 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so February 2100 has
+28 days.
+
+The other century tests in the same file agree with this rule:
+
+- 1900 -> 28 (not a leap year)
+- 2000 -> 29 (leap year)
+
+Making `test_feb_2100` pass would require encoding the false claim that 2100 is a
+leap year, which would also break `month_end()` for February 2100 and contradict
+the correct century behavior the suite otherwise checks. I did not work around
+it.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`is_leap()` now implements the full Gregorian rule:
+
+```python
+def is_leap(year):
+    return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)
+```
+
+This fixes the original bug (years like 1900 were incorrectly treated as leap
+years) and makes every test pass except `test_feb_2100`.
+
+## What needs to change
+
+The test, not the implementation. `test_feb_2100` should assert 28 days, e.g.:
+
+```python
+def test_feb_2100(self):
+    # 2100 is divisible by 100 but not 400, so it is not a leap year.
+    self.assertEqual(days_in_month(2100, 2), 28)
+```
